// A float displays the same way on both engines, whatever its magnitude.
//
// Compiled code used to format floats in C with "%.*g" at the fewest digits
// that round-tripped. %g switches to exponent form as soon as the exponent
// reaches the precision, which is exactly when a float needs trailing zeros
// before the decimal point — so `jade build` printed 1e+01 for 10.0 and 4.8e+04
// for 48000.0 while `jade run` printed 10.0 and 48000.0. Sample rates, byte
// counts and durations were the values that broke; 1024.0 and 10.5 looked fine,
// which is what kept it hidden. Both engines now share one renderer.

// The values that broke.
print(10.0)
print(100.0)
print(110.0)
print(48000.0)
print(44100.0)
print(-10.0)
// → 10.0
// → 100.0
// → 110.0
// → 48000.0
// → 44100.0
// → -10.0

// The neighbours that always worked, so a regression shows as a change rather
// than a whole category going quiet.
print(1024.0)
print(10.5)
print(0.001)
print(0.0)
// → 1024.0
// → 10.5
// → 0.001
// → 0.0

// str() and f-strings take the same path as print.
print(str(48000.0))
print(f"rate {44100.0} hz")
// → 48000.0
// → rate 44100.0 hz

// Inside a container, which was always correct — the two paths must agree.
print([10.0, 20.0])
// → [10.0, 20.0]
